The '''2014 UEFA Super Cup''' was the 39th edition of the [[UEFA Super Cup]], an annual [[association football|football]] match organised by [[UEFA]] and contested by the reigning champions of the two main [[Europe]]an club competitions, the [[UEFA Champions League]] and the [[UEFA Europa League]]. The match featured two Spanish teams [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] and [[Sevilla FC|Sevilla]], the winners of the [[2013–14 UEFA Champions League]] and the [[2013–14 UEFA Europa League]] respectively.<ref>{{cite news |title=Cardiff date for Madrid and Sevilla |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefasupercup/news/0250-0c510566447b-a3e6782c1f72-1000--cardiff-date-for-madrid-and-sevilla/ |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=24 May 2014 }}</ref> It was played at the [[Cardiff City Stadium]] in [[Cardiff]], Wales,<ref name="decision">{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefasupercup/news/0250-0c50fb8f7dbe-0d15b1a19f6c-1000--uefa-euro-2020-uefa-super-cup-decisions/ |title=UEFA EURO 2020, UEFA Super Cup decisions |website=UEFA.org |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=30 June 2012 }}</ref> on 12 August 2014.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/0253-0d7eeaa71d98-7ddf54a81673-1000--strategic-talks-in-dubrovnik/ |title=Strategic talks in Dubrovnik |website=UEFA.org |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=20 September 2013 }}</ref> The date was moved from Friday in late August in previous years, to mid-August starting this year, following the removal of the August international friendly date in the new [[FIFA International Match Calendar]].<ref name="decision"/>

Real Madrid won 2–0 to win their second UEFA Super Cup, with both goals by [[Cristiano Ronaldo]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/28741099 |title=Super Cup: Cristiano Ronaldo scores twice in Real Madrid win |date=12 August 2014 |work=[[BBC Sport]] |access-date=19 August 2014 }}</ref>

==Venue==
[[File:UEFA Super Cup, Cardiff 2014.jpg|thumb|alt=Opening ceremony for the 2014 UEFA Super cup final in Cardiff, UK.|The match was played at the [[Cardiff City Stadium]] in Cardiff, Wales.]]
The [[Cardiff City Stadium]] was announced as the venue of the Super Cup at the UEFA Executive Committee meeting on 30 June 2012.<ref name="decision"/> This was the first UEFA Super Cup hosted in Wales.

The Cardiff City Stadium opened in July 2009 on the site of the [[Cardiff Athletics Stadium]]. It is the home stadium of [[Cardiff City F.C.|Cardiff City]]. The stadium had a capacity of 33,000 after expansion work.<ref>{{cite news |title=Cardiff to embrace UEFA Super Cup |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefasupercup/news/0250-0c51058da6eb-a92eadf2d40b-1000--cardiff-to-embrace-uefa-super-cup/ |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=17 February 2014 }}</ref>

